# Does collapsing the four enzyme fractions to ONE shared value help?
#
# The design becomes 1 + n coordinates -- a single master mix split across a temperature gradient,
# which is how the assay is actually run -- instead of 2n. The invariant kernel then composes
# exactly as symmetricRBF(temperatures) * RBF(fraction): one lengthscale tied across the
# temperatures (a symmetric function cannot tell experiment 1 from experiment 3), one for the
# fraction, which is now a single genuinely distinguishable coordinate.
#
# The shared-fraction space is the DIAGONAL of the full space where all fractions are equal, so the
# objective is the same function restricted to a subspace -- losses compare directly with the 8-dim
# arm in output/wide/w5-*, which ran the same box and the same read-out noise.
shared |both|--kernel permutation-invariant-rbf --shared-fraction
